From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: every monitor shows the desk at its own density

The desk is one page, rastered at the densest monitor's scale and shown
on every monitor, each shrinking it to its own. Left there, a 1x monitor
beside a 2x one shows text rastered at 2 and halved.

So a page is told every monitor it is shown on -- the browser lists
them among its screens, labeled -- and a widget turns them into regions
of its viewport, in device pixels, each with the monitor's scale over
the page's. A picture layer drawn straight into the page keeps, on the
active tree, a tiling at its raster scale times each ratio it meets:
rastered only where that monitor shows it, after the page's own tiles in
every bin, and never cleaned up as non-ideal while the monitor shows it.
AppendQuads draws each region from that tiling, falling back to the
page's own tiles where one is not ready, so viz's shrink lands every
texel on one of the monitor's pixels.

See cc/domicile/display_regions.h.
